# L §5.11 step 5: "If a rule of unknown applicability could change the outcome, the result is a hole that carries
# that when's questions. It could change the outcome when it would beat a candidate, be a candidate beside the
# winner, or be the only rule left."  k1: rule exc (hole when) would beat base: a hole.  k2: exc is beaten by
# the applicable rule top, so it can't change the outcome: top wins (value 1).
=== m.tally
model t
  tally 2
  on 2026-01-01
  start 2026-01-01
  end 2026-12-31

source s "s"
index ks = [k1, k2]
input f: bool
input g[k in ks]: bool

def x[k in ks]: int at reader
  rule base = 0
  rule exc beats base = -1 when f
  rule top beats exc, base = 1 when g[k]

version v
  cite s
  governs always
  known 2026-01-01
  f = ?"does the exception hold?"
  g[k1] = false
  g[k2] = true

ask q1: x[k] for k in ks law 2026-06-30
=== expect
q1 (k = k1): ?"does the exception hold?" (f)
  law date 2026-06-30, cut 2026-01-01
q1 (k = k2): 1
  law date 2026-06-30, cut 2026-01-01
